chunks.text:
The Navy Malaria Department concentrates on developing a vaccine for the malaria parasite. She is often asked why would the Navy or any of the armed forces be interested in infectious disease? Her answer is easy: protection for the troops. For example, in 2011 there were 124 cases of malaria, mostly in Afghanistan. Infectious diseases have a direct impact on our military and their ability to carry out their mission.

Currently, 51 microbiologists are serving the Navy in active duty around the world, including five research laboratories (Cairo; Lima, Peru;

Phnom Penh, Cambodia; Silver Spring, Md.; and San Diego). The overseas labs also have areas of concentration specific to the infectious disease found in that area and function in coordination with the local Ministries of Health, academies, and sometimes, active non-governmental organizations.

